From ub5aef0648e455158 at groups.google.com  Wed Jan 14 04:05:17 2015
From: ub5aef0648e455158 at groups.google.com (Rick Gerkin)
Date: Wed, 14 Jan 2015 04:05:17 +0000
Subject: Announcing sklearn-pystan
Message-ID: <UE5G5wpaU9k.NYFTT8C7joUJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/UE5G5wpaU9k/m/NYFTT8C7joUJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

scikit-learn is a python library for all sorts of statistics and machine learning work.  One thing I like about it is the ease with which things like parameter searches and cross-validation can be implemented.  I had been using it (manually) to do some model checking of Stan models, and have recently created a new library here:

https://github.com/rgerkin/pystan-sklearn

to illustrate how this can be done in a slightly more formal way.  Unfortunately it requires some manual implementation for each model (I don't have an automated way to extract from the Stan model how goodness-of-fit should be scored, for example), but that's a small price to pay if you are going to run a big batch fit with a large number of parameters and want to do it efficiently.

Let me know if you all have any ideas for how this could be made better, faster, cleaner, or if this is even useful to anyone else.

Rick

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Wed Jan 14 20:45:29 2015
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Wed, 14 Jan 2015 20:45:29 +0000
Subject: out of domain errors (e.g., with CDFs)
Message-ID: <7raBwR3LomY.jRmPB8xO0yUJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/7raBwR3LomY/m/jRmPB8xO0yUJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

We have the general problem that for many functions, the mathematical
range exceeds the floating-point range, either for the function itself
or for its derivative.

For example, the normal CDF only works in roughly the range (-25,25)
or so.  But the limit for the value is 0 to the left and 1
to the right, with a limit of 0 for the derivatives.  For the inverse gamma
CDF where Rob ran into this issue, as y->0, the value goes to 0 and the
derivative to +infinity.

I think the problem should be framed in terms of impliciations for
optimization and HMC.  For gradient based optimization, whatever
parameter hits a 0 derivative won't move.  In HMC, it'll just have
0 potential, but it'll still move because of the random kinetic
energy --- but it'll never make a U-turn.  In both, infinite values
or derivatives wind up leaving you stuck where you're at.

QUESTION:  What should we do when called with a var/fvar out of domain?

1.  Raise an exception.

2.  Return whatever the limit is (+inf, -inf, 0).

3.  Sometimes 1, sometimes 2, depending on the limit in question.

4.  None of the above;  we should _____________.

I believe whatever we do we want to doc it in general and carry it through
all of our functions.

- Bob

