MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

No, it wasn't. It came from using 'const std::string&' in place of 'const char*'.

I went through an cleaned up a lot of the error handling. We thought it'd be a good idea to just use string instead of char*. Turns out it impacted performance a lot. It had to construct a lot of strings, which ended up hurting performance. We could have created a static const string in the function and that has similar performance as using char* directly, but we thought it'd be programmer-error prone, so I went back to char*.

Daniel

On Mon, Jan 26, 2015 at 2:03 PM, Allen B. Riddell <a...@ariddell.org> wrote:
> Out of curiosity, what was the source of the problem? It wasn't the
> header-only change, right?
>
> On 01/26, Daniel Lee wrote:
> > So, after finding the slow-down, I took a couple days to add a test that
> > runs a simple model 100 times and records the output. Each run is about 3
> > seconds, so it takes 5 minutes to run.
> >
> > I ran it on the Jenkins machine over each of the merge commits since v2.5.0
> > (including v2.5.0).
> >
> > I've plotted the time range in gray, added 50% intervals in black, and the
> > average as a circle. I'm going to create a pull request to add this test
> > into our development branch and I'll create a new job for Jenkins to run
> > every pull request and every merge. I don't know how to have a running plot
> > in Jenkins, but we'll figure it out.
> >
> > [image: Inline image 1]
> >
> >
> > I've also attached the raw data in case you wanted to see it. If you think
> > it should be doing something else, please let me know.
> >
> >
> >
> >
> >
> > Daniel
